  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSatellite shows low clouds melting away from SoCal offshore at \nthis hour. Mostly clear conditions will continue through the \nafternoon, before low clouds move back inland this evening. High \npressure anchored over New Mexico will strengthen and expand into \nthe weekend. This will keep the marine layer thinner, confining \nlow clouds within 10 miles from the coast each night and morning \nfor much of the upcoming week.\n\nThe high's growth and transition westward will also bring a \nwarmer weather to the area through the weekend. The warming trend\nbegins on Thursday with temperatures rising another 1-3 degrees \nacross the area. By Friday, the high strengthens right around 600 \ndm, indicating a greater increase in temperatures (this time, \nanother 3-5 degrees warmer). The weather system peaks in intensity\non Saturday with high temperatures around 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal. Widespread highs of 100 to locally near 110 can be \nexpected across the Inland Empire and High Desert, potentially \nnearing 120 in the lower deserts. Widespread 80s and 90s will be \nfelt across the mountains, western valleys and beaches. Overnight\nlow temperatures will also be warm as more humid conditions \nremain. Please practice good heat safety by limiting outdoor \nrecreation (mountains included!), taking water breaks, and seeking\nshade when you are able to do so.\n\nThe high's strength begins to weaken slightly by Sunday into early\nnext week. Sunday's temperatures will be similar to Saturday\nacross mountains and deserts, cooling slightly for areas west of\nthe mountains as an increased sea breeze tries to cool things off\na bit. High pressure remains over the desert southwest, albeit\nweaker, for the first half of next week. This will cool coastal\nareas and western valleys off closer to normal. Inland \ntemperatures across mountains and deserts will remain elevated \nnearing ten degrees above normal; chances remain high for \ntemperatures to stay above 110 degrees across the lower deserts at\nthis time. Increases in low level moisture is also seen for \ninland regions by the middle of next week, so chances for rain and\nstorms remain small, but increase slightly.",
